<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Sino-Japanese War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Sino-Japanese War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1937 to 1945. Beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident">Marco Polo Bridge Incident</a>, Japan occupied much of eastern China, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, where its troops committed a notorious massacre; Nationalists and Communists resisted, and from 1941 the war merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, ending with Japan's surrender in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war.jpg" alt="Second Sino-Japanese War" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer, Ministry of the Navy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Special_Naval_Landing_Forces_in_Battle_of_Shanghai_1937.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war followed a long period of Japanese expansion on the Chinese mainland. After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> of 1894–1895, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1905 it held the South Manchuria Railway Zone and the Kwantung Leased Territory. On 18 September 1931 Japanese officers staged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> incident, a false flag event used to justify the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the creation of the puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>.</p><p>Inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Nationalist forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> and Communist forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> had been fighting each other since 1927. Chiang's policy was first internal pacification, then external resistance, and his encirclement campaigns drove the Communists onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>. As a Japanese invasion became imminent, Chiang formed a United Front with the Communists in 1936, a process hastened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident">Xi'an Incident</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war is dated from the night of 7 July 1937, when Chinese and Japanese troops exchanged fire near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Marco Polo Bridge</a>, about 16 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. The skirmishing grew into full-scale invasion. After the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shanghai">Battle of Shanghai</a>, Japanese forces took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1937 and committed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing-massacre">Nanjing Massacre</a>. China had little military-industrial strength and no mechanised divisions. The Nationalist government withdrew into the interior and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> their wartime seat, trading space for time, while Communist forces waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare in the north.</p><p>By 1939 the war had reached a stalemate, with Japanese supply lines stretched deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> in December 1941 the conflict merged with the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> became China's main financial and military supporter. Supplies came over the Burma Road and, once it was cut, by air over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>. In 1944 Japan launched Operation Ichi-Go, its largest campaign of the war, which mobilised 500,000 troops and cost Chinese armies about 300,000 casualties. In 1945 Chinese forces retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> formally surrendered on 2 September 1945, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> and the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, and the surrender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 9 September. Estimates of Chinese deaths range from 10 million to 20 million, with a common figure of 15 to 16 million, most of them civilians. Japan used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a> and carried out large biological warfare attacks, and its scorched earth policies in North and Central China killed millions of civilians. The war produced about 95 million refugees.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> emerged as one of the Allied great powers, regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and became a permanent member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a>. It was also economically weak, with ruined farmland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and famine, and it was on the verge of renewed civil war between the two sides.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Sino-Japanese War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Sino-Japanese War</b> she became a symbol of unity and of resistance to Japan's aggression, notably in the 1939 film Mulan Joins the Army, directed by Bu Wancang and written by Ouyang Yuqian.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ballad-of-mulan">The Ballad of Mulan</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Second Sino-Japanese War</b> the march was sung well beyond the Communist Party: the Nationalist general Dai Anlan made it the anthem of the 200th Division, which fought in Burma.</blockquote><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a></li><li><blockquote>The modern lyrics, attributed to Li Youyuan, a farmer from Shaanbei (northern Shaanxi), were produced in 1942 during the <b>Second Sino-Japanese War</b>; he is said to have been inspired by the rising sun.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Sino-Japanese War</b> merged into the Second World War and cost millions of Chinese lives.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-7">7 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
